Sculpt and rig the **Sunfront Trooper**, an armored Duneforged infantry soldier
whose whole plated body deforms as one continuous skin — as a {{voxel.width}}x{{voxel.height}}x{{voxel.depth}} hard-surface
model using only the `dc-skin` tool. You build it by **sculpting one whole-body
signed-distance field** — one recorded operation at a time — bind it to a skeleton you
invent, and **author its three animations** as F-curves.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief
tells you *what the Sunfront Trooper is* and *how it must move*, but the field you
sculpt, the skeleton you build, where you place the joints, and how you shape every
form are **entirely yours to invent**. Work out the pieces an armored, marching,
firing soldier needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to sculpt the character and edit the skeleton is the `dc-skin` binary
already on your `PATH`. `dc-skin` builds a **single, whole-body signed-distance field**
— the entire trooper at once, meshed into **one continuous surface** by Dual
Contouring, which keeps sharp edges and corners for hard-surface armored plate. There
is **no `--part` flag**: unlike a per-part rig, a skinned character is **one field with
one log**, and its skin deforms **across** its joints. The ordered operations you issue
— recorded to `actions.json` — together with the skeleton and animations you define are
your submission.

You sculpt the field with CSG primitives — additive and subtractive spheres, boxes,
ellipsoids, and cylinders, their soft `--blend` union, and `clear` — each an opaque
`#rrggbb` `--color` (there is no transparency). The field starts **empty**. Each call
only records the operation and renders **nothing** — run `dc-skin render` to (re)draw
the preview `model.png` and, before you finish, to **emit the geometry the run is
scored from** (an unrendered model is scored as empty). `dc-skin render --time <ms>
--animation <name>` renders the character **posed with actual skin deformation** at
that instant, so you can watch a joint actually fold.

**You invent the skeleton.** Define bones in a hierarchy with `define-bone` (the first
is the root) and position each head→tail with `set-bone`; place joints on them with
`define-joint`; and author the required animations with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. The **per-vertex skin weights are derived
automatically** at `render` by bone-heat diffusion (up to four bones per vertex) — you
do not paint them operation-by-operation, though `paint-weight` can override a region
the automatic result gets wrong (for example pinning a rigid helmet fully to the head
bone).

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, for example:

```
dc-skin add-box --x 3 --y 4 --z 2 --w 4 --h 5 --d 3 --color "#b07a35"
dc-skin define-bone --name pelvis --parent ""
```

(The names and numbers above are just examples — build your own field and skeleton
however you like.) Run `dc-skin --help` to list every operation **and** the skeleton
and animation subcommands (defining and positioning bones, placing joints, and
authoring animations with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `dc-skin <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract.
**Read `model.png` after you run `dc-skin render`** to see the character at rest, and
render a **posed** preview (`dc-skin render --time <ms> --animation <name>`) to confirm
the skin actually deforms across the joints.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Sculpt and rig the trooper up operation by operation until `model.png` matches the
brief and the skeleton is complete: an armored humanoid whose one continuous skin binds
to a skeleton and deforms convincingly across its joints. **Author the three required
animations** as F-curves with the `dc-skin` animation subcommands, easing each so a
heavy, plated body carries weight rather than sliding linearly:

- **`march`** — a game-triggered playable that **loops**: a purposeful two-phase walk,
  each foot planting and holding while the body passes over it, then lifting, swinging,
  and planting, the two legs in opposite phase with the arms counter-swinging. Author
  it **in place** — the leg cycle carries the stride; a game supplies the real travel.
  The skin folds across the hips, knees, and waist through the cycle.
- **`fire`** — a game-triggered playable that plays **once**: shoulder the rifle,
  brace, and take a single shot whose recoil kicks back through the shoulder and torso
  and settles. The skin folds at the shoulder and elbow; the legs hold planted.
- **`brace`** — a game-triggered playable that plays **once and holds**: drop into a
  low crouch behind cover, knees bending deeply, hips and spine flexing forward and the
  head tucking down, then hold the crouched pose.

The three animations are the fixed contract — produce them, by those names; the bones
and joints that realize them are yours. **Run `dc-skin render` before you finish** so
the skinned `mesh.glb` and `rig.json` are emitted. When you are satisfied, stop: the
recorded operation log and `rig.json` are your finished submission.
